/**
 * @defgroup optimal_pump_efficiency_calculator Corrected Average Pump Efficiency
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief Combines HI 2020 base efficiency and speed correction with the viscosity adjustment.
 * @details
 * What average efficiency is achievable for this pump style and duty? The result is an engineering
 * estimate used when a measured curve is unavailable, returned as a dimensionless efficiency fraction.
 *
 * @heading{Final Result and Decision Path}
 * Select the HI 2020 base curve and interpolate the flow. Convert percent to fraction, apply the
 * retained viscosity multiplier, and subtract the specific-speed decrement.
 *
 * @formula{hi2020-corrected-average;
 * \eta_{average}=\frac{E_{pre}}{100}C_{\nu}-C_{Ns}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta_{average}; Corrected average efficiency fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{E_{pre}; HI base efficiency; \percent}
 * @symrow{100; Percentage-point scale per fraction; \percent}
 * @symrow{C_{\nu}; Viscosity multiplier capped at one; \unitless}
 * @symrow{C_{Ns}; Fractional specific-speed decrement; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * @formula{hi2020-viscosity-parameter;
 * B=26.6\frac{\nu^{0.5}H^{0.0625}}{Q^{0.375}n^{0.25}},\qquad
 * C_{\nu}=\min\left(1,B^{-0.0547B^{0.69}}\right)
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{B; Empirical viscosity parameter in the stated numerical conventions; \unitless}
 * @symrow{\nu; Liquid kinematic viscosity; cSt}
 * @symrow{H; Total head across all stages; \foot}
 * @symrow{Q; Flow in the US gallon convention; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @symrow{n; Pump rotational speed; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{26.6; Empirical scale for the stated mixed-unit convention; mixed-unit empirical scale}
 * @symrow{0.0547; Empirical exponent coefficient; \unitless}
 * @symrow{0.69; Empirical exponent; \unitless}
 * @symrow{0.5,0.0625,0.375,0.25; Empirical viscosity-parameter exponents; \unitless}
 * @symrow{C_{\nu}; Viscosity multiplier; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The viscosity relationship and specific-speed definition are retained. Specific speed uses head
 * per stage; see @ref optimal_specific_speed_calculator. Base and speed corrections now use the
 * engineering HI 20.3-2020 tables via @ref optimal_pre_pump_efficiency_calculator and
 * @ref optimal_specific_speed_correction_calculator. Viscosity correction occurs once.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Example}
 * An ANSI/API pump at Q=2000 US gpm, n=2000 rpm, H=137 ft, one stage and nu=1.107 cSt has
 * base efficiency approximately 83.99065%. Its viscosity multiplier is capped at 1.
 * Specific speed is approximately 2233.596 in the US convention; the speed correction is
 * approximately 0.00708346 percentage points, or 0.0000708346 as a fraction. The result is
 * approximately 0.8398357, equivalent to 83.98357% average efficiency.
 *
 * @heading{Assumptions and Availability}
 * Inputs must be finite, with positive rpm/head/stage count, nonnegative viscosity, and flow and
 * derived specific speed inside the inclusive reference ranges. Fractional positive stage counts
 * remain numerically supported. Out-of-range, invalid or unsupported duties return NaN.
 * Specified efficiency and positive displacement use supplied-efficiency paths elsewhere.
 * No final output clamp is applied; extreme viscosity can yield a nonpositive estimate, which must
 * not be used as a usable pump efficiency. These reference ranges concern estimate availability,
 * not whether equipment can operate or whether a measured assessment can be calculated.
 *
 * @see optimal_pump_efficiency::calculate
 */
